Ladybird now closes its UDP connection to the configured DNS server
when a query is due for a retry and nothing has come back on the
connection since the query went out on it, and sends the query again on
a new connection. Chrome’s DNS client never reuses one: Its
DnsTransactionImpl::MakeUdpAttempt() asks for a new, randomly bound
socket for each attempt, so a retry always goes out from the address
the network has at that moment. Chrome also aborts in-flight lookups
and clears its caches in HostResolverManager::OnIPAddressChanged(),
which Ladybird has no counterpart to. Gecko sends no DNS over UDP
itself; its plain lookups go through the system resolver, whose
getaddrinfo() opens a socket for each lookup. Those are from reading
the sources. The failure was checked in a Linux network namespace: A
UDP socket connected to 1.1.1.1 kept its Wi-Fi source address and kept
sending without error after the default route moved to another
interface, while a new socket took that interface’s address.
